From: Adriel Santos <adriel@adriel.eu>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath11k: don't nest the IRQ disable of a failed recovery

The firmware crash path disables the data path and CE interrupts at the
start of ath11k_core_reconfigure_on_crash() and enables them again in
ath11k_core_qmi_firmware_ready() and ath11k_core_start(). disable_irq()
nests, though: an IRQ disabled twice needs two enable_irq() calls, and
the recovery makes one. When a recovery fails, its interrupts stay
disabled (never enabled, or disabled again by ath11k_hif_stop() in the
unwind), the next recovery disables them a second time, and then:

- on AHB (IPQ5018), __ath11k_ahb_ext_irq_disable() nests: the recovery
  succeeds with every data path interrupt still off;

- on the pcic ops (QCN6122, and PCI chips on the reset path),
  __ath11k_pcic_ext_irq_disable() nests the same way, and the CE
  interrupts nest between ath11k_pci_disable_ce_irqs_except_wake_irq()
  and ath11k_pcic_ce_irqs_disable() on one side and the single
  ath11k_pcic_ce_irqs_enable() on the other, so the recovery never gets
  its WMI ready event ("failed to start core: -110").

On an IPQ5018/QCN6122 AX3000T both radios stayed down this way when the
firmware crashed again during recovery and the user PDs were restarted
afterwards.

Leave interrupts that are already disabled alone. On AHB napi_enabled
tracks the ext IRQ state: the IRQs are requested with IRQ_NOAUTOEN and
napi_enabled clear, ath11k_ahb_ext_irq_enable() sets it and the disable
clears it. The pcic ops already keep ATH11K_FLAG_EXT_IRQ_ENABLED and
ATH11K_FLAG_CE_IRQ_ENABLED; use them, and keep the CE flag in the
_except_wake_irq() variants too when the chip cannot suspend, where
they cover every CE. A chip that can suspend spares its wake CE there,
so its CE handling is unchanged.

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/ahb.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/ahb.c
@@ -244,13 +244,17 @@
 	for (i = 0; i < ATH11K_EXT_IRQ_GRP_NUM_MAX; i++) {
 		struct ath11k_ext_irq_grp *irq_grp = &ab->ext_irq_grp[i];
 
+		/* Already disabled. disable_irq() nests, and the single
+		 * ath11k_ahb_ext_irq_enable() would leave the group off.
+		 */
+		if (!irq_grp->napi_enabled)
+			continue;
+
 		ath11k_ahb_ext_grp_disable(irq_grp);
 
-		if (irq_grp->napi_enabled) {
-			napi_synchronize(&irq_grp->napi);
-			napi_disable(&irq_grp->napi);
-			irq_grp->napi_enabled = false;
-		}
+		napi_synchronize(&irq_grp->napi);
+		napi_disable(&irq_grp->napi);
+		irq_grp->napi_enabled = false;
 	}
 }
 
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/pcic.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/pcic.c
@@ -389,7 +389,12 @@
 {
 	int i;
 
-	clear_bit(ATH11K_FLAG_CE_IRQ_ENABLED, &ab->dev_flags);
+	/* already disabled: disable_irq() nests, a single enable would not
+	 * undo two (see ath11k_pci_disable_ce_irqs_except_wake_irq())
+	 */
+	if (!test_and_clear_bit(ATH11K_FLAG_CE_IRQ_ENABLED, &ab->dev_flags) &&
+	    !ab->hw_params.supports_suspend)
+		return;
 
 	for (i = 0; i < ab->hw_params.ce_count; i++) {
 		if (ath11k_ce_get_attr_flags(ab, i) & CE_ATTR_DIS_INTR)
@@ -460,7 +465,11 @@
 {
 	int i;
 
-	clear_bit(ATH11K_FLAG_EXT_IRQ_ENABLED, &ab->dev_flags);
+	/* already disabled: disable_irq() nests, and the single
+	 * ath11k_pcic_ext_irq_enable() would leave the groups off
+	 */
+	if (!test_and_clear_bit(ATH11K_FLAG_EXT_IRQ_ENABLED, &ab->dev_flags))
+		return;
 
 	for (i = 0; i < ATH11K_EXT_IRQ_GRP_NUM_MAX; i++) {
 		struct ath11k_ext_irq_grp *irq_grp = &ab->ext_irq_grp[i];
@@ -843,6 +852,11 @@
 {
 	int i;
 
+	/* no wake CE to spare: this is ath11k_pcic_ce_irqs_enable(), flag included */
+	if (!ab->hw_params.supports_suspend &&
+	    test_and_set_bit(ATH11K_FLAG_CE_IRQ_ENABLED, &ab->dev_flags))
+		return;
+
 	for (i = 0; i < ab->hw_params.ce_count; i++) {
 		if (ath11k_ce_get_attr_flags(ab, i) & CE_ATTR_DIS_INTR)
 			continue;
@@ -861,6 +875,18 @@
 	int irq_idx;
 	struct ath11k_ce_pipe *ce_pipe;
 
+	/*
+	 * A chip that cannot suspend has no wake CE to spare, so this disables
+	 * every CE, as ath11k_pcic_ce_irqs_disable() does: keep that function's
+	 * flag, and leave CEs that are already disabled alone. A crash recovery
+	 * that fails before ath11k_pcic_start(), or in ath11k_pcic_stop(),
+	 * leaves them disabled, and the next recovery would otherwise disable
+	 * them again and never get its WMI ready event.
+	 */
+	if (!ab->hw_params.supports_suspend &&
+	    !test_and_clear_bit(ATH11K_FLAG_CE_IRQ_ENABLED, &ab->dev_flags))
+		return;
+
 	for (i = 0; i < ab->hw_params.ce_count; i++) {
 		ce_pipe = &ab->ce.ce_pipe[i];
 		irq_idx = ATH11K_PCI_IRQ_CE0_OFFSET + i;
